  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Solve ${b}^{3}-2{b}^{2}-6b+12=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{3}-2{b}^{2}-6b+12 & = \\\\ ({b}^{3}-2{b}^{2})-(6b-12) & = \\\\ {b}^{2}(b-2)-6(b-2) & = \\\\ (b-2)\\cdot {b}^{2}-(b-2)\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ (b-2)({b}^{2}-6) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(b-2)({b}^{2}-6)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(b-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({b}^{2}-6)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {b}^{2} & =6 \\\\ b & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $b=2,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{6}.$",
